{"name":"Singing the Blues","hex":"#2b4d68","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"singing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of using the voice to produce musical sounds; vocalizing.","Disclosing information, or giving evidence about another."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Smooth and flowing.","Producing a whistling sound due to the escape of steam."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/singing"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Singing the Blues","blocks":[{"text":"\"Singing the Blues\" is a popular song composed by Melvin Endsley and published in 1956. The highest-charting version was by Guy Mitchell and the first recording of the song was by Marty Robbins. It is not related to the 1920 jazz song \"Singin' the Blues\" recorded by Frank Trumbauer and Bix Beiderbecke in 1927."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/US_78_singing_the_blues_guy_mitchell.jpg/330px-US_78_singing_the_blues_guy_mitchell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singing_the_Blues","wikidata":"Q3372814"},{"title":"Singing the Blues (Big Joe Turner album)","blocks":[{"text":"Singing the Blues is an album by blues vocalist Joe Turner recorded in 1967 and originally released by the BluesWay label."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1b/Singing_the_Blues_%28Big_Joe_Turner_album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singing_the_Blues_(Big_Joe_Turner_album)","wikidata":"Q85801303"},{"title":"Singing","blocks":[{"text":"Singing is the art of creating music with the voice. It is the oldest form of musical expression, and the human voice can be considered the first musical instrument by some. The definition of singing varies across sources. Some sources define singing as the act of creating musical sounds with the voice. Other common definitions include \"the utterance of words or sounds in tuneful succession\" or \"the production of musical tones by means of the human voice\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Ah_cricket_20122_%287364759010%29.jpg/330px-Ah_cricket_20122_%287364759010%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singing","wikidata":"Q27939"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues","wikidata":"Q9759"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Windsbacher_d.jpg/500px-Windsbacher_d.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Windsbacher_d.jpg/500px-Windsbacher_d.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boys' choir.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindsbacher_d.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Hals%2C_Frans_-_Singing_Girl_-_1626-30.png/500px-Hals%2C_Frans_-_Singing_Girl_-_1626-30.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Hals%2C_Frans_-_Singing_Girl_-_1626-30.png/500px-Hals%2C_Frans_-_Singing_Girl_-_1626-30.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Girl Singing (Frans Hals, about 1628)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHals%2C_Frans_-_Singing_Girl_-_1626-30.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Gray1204.png/500px-Gray1204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Gray1204.png/500px-Gray1204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray1204.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg/500px-F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg/1280px-F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg/500px-F%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"People singing together at Bella Center in Copenhagen, 2025","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AF%C3%A6llessang%2C_Bogforum_2025.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Illu01_head_neck.jpg/330px-Illu01_head_neck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai